  "description": "<p>It would be helpful to have a standard way to use the Camel Toolbox to facilitate messaging between Fedora and arbitrary third-party microservices/APIs.</p>\n\n<p>For example: my use case is that whenever an object is added or modified in Fedora, I would like to POST the object's identifier to a web service that handles indexing. (My indexing process is quite complex and depends on other parts of my non-Java-based codebase, and so it makes more sense to handle it as an external microservice rather than trying to directly incorporate the whole indexing process into the Camel Toolbox).</p>\n\n<p>It seems that it may be possible to create a configurable service in the toolbox which could meet my use case while also being flexible enough to support a significant number of other possible use cases. For example, if we could configure which types of actions trigger a message, the target URL, the HTTP method to use, and the payload (which could potentially be GET and/or POST parameters, or a message body).</p>\n\n<p>I have full control over the nature of my own web service, so I can tailor the code I build to suit whatever implementation is easiest on the Camel Toolbox side &#8211; thus, I do not demand all this flexibility... but it might be helpful to others if easy to implement. I would be perfectly happy to start with a minimum viable product that is designed to be extensible in case of future need.</p>\n\n<p>If others feel this is a good idea and would like to discuss a path forward, I am willing to lead an initial implementation, on the understanding that I'm unfamiliar with the platform and might need a little hand-holding to get things underway. <img class=\"emoticon\" src=\"/images/icons/emoticons/smile.png\" height=\"16\" width=\"16\" align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" border=\"0\"/></p>\n\n<p>This topic was discussed on the August 12, 2021 Fedora Tech call.</p>",
